{:edb/db-before-t 2280 :edb/db-after-t 2281 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535593 50 #inst "2026-10-09T03:10:30.053Z" 13194139535593 true] [17592186092183 1020 #edb/ref 17592186356683 13194139535593 true] [17592186092183 1020 #edb/ref 17592186356694 13194139535593 true] [17592186092258 1020 #edb/ref 17592186356687 13194139535593 true] [17592186092260 1020 #edb/ref 17592186356690 13194139535593 true] [17592186092706 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535593 true] [17592186092706 1100 "Find the slope of the tangent line to the polar curve $r = \\ln θ$, at $θ = π$, given that\n\n$y^{′}(θ) = \\frac{1}{θ}\\sin θ + \\ln θ\\cos θ, x^{′}(θ) = \\frac{1}{θ}\\cos θ - \\ln θ\\sin θ$." 13194139535593 false] [17592186092706 1100 "Find the slope of the tangent line to the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta $, at $\\theta =\\pi $,  given that \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(\\theta )=\\frac{1}{\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\,{x}^{′}(\\theta )=\\frac{1}{\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$" 13194139535593 true] [17592186092706 1145 "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the rule for differentiating parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the numerator and denominator at $\\theta =\\pi ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\pi ) & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi +\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\pi \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot 0+\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\pi \\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\pi ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\pi ) & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\pi \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot (-1)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\pi \\cdot 0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, the slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =\\pi } & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\pi )}{{x}^{′}(\\pi )} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\pi }{(-\\frac{1}{\\pi })} \\\\  & =\\pi \\operatorname{ln}⁡\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535593 true] [17592186356683 1017 #edb/ref 17592186356684 13194139535593 true] [17592186356683 1089 "q-348107" 13194139535593 true] [17592186356683 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535593 true] [17592186356683 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068032/assets/70c737e1fbbf63b3551763e48f4d7a956ffcc04ff68cced4f813b28e87630eb3.png)\n\nFor the polar curve $r=\\frac{{e}^{2\\theta }}{2\\pi },$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ as a function of $\\theta .$\n\n| $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ | $=$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | $2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ |" 13194139535593 true] [17592186356683 1113 #uuid "72209b1c-5622-5751-b8d7-9f75e7528fa7" 13194139535593 true] [17592186356683 1145 "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r=\\frac{{e}^{2\\theta }}{2\\pi },$ we get\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{{e}^{2\\theta }}{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\frac{{e}^{2\\theta }}{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTo compute our derivative, we first need to calculate ${x}^{′}(\\theta )$ and ${y}^{′}(\\theta ).$ \n\n- First, we compute the derivative of $x$ with respect to $\\theta $ using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={(\\frac{{e}^{2\\theta }}{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }{({e}^{2\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }[({e}^{2\\theta })^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{e}^{2\\theta }\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{′}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }[2{e}^{2\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{e}^{2\\theta }\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }[2{e}^{2\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -{e}^{2\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ] \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2\\theta }}{2\\pi }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute the derivative of $y$ with respect to $\\theta $ using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={(\\frac{{e}^{2\\theta }}{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }{({e}^{2\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }[({e}^{2\\theta })^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{e}^{2\\theta }\\cdot (\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{′}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }[2{e}^{2\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{e}^{2\\theta }\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }[2{e}^{2\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{e}^{2\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ] \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2\\theta }}{2\\pi }(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{{e}^{2\\theta }}{2\\pi }(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}{\\frac{{e}^{2\\theta }}{2\\pi }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535593 true] [17592186356684 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356685 13194139535593 true] [17592186356684 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356686 13194139535593 true] [17592186356684 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535593 true] [17592186356684 